"[2610.22428v1] Embeddings Are Overpriced? A Frozen 16 MB Vocabulary for Small and Ternary Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22428v1)\nciteturn41216view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22428v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:Embeddings Are Overpriced? A Frozen 16 MB Vocabulary for Small and Ternary Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Sai Kiran Bathula L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Embeddings Are Overpriced? A Frozen 16 MB Vocabulary for Small and Ternary Language Models, by Sai Kiran Bathula\nL14: \nL15: cite6†View PDF cite7†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:In a small language model the vocabulary is the single largest part. In Gemma 3 270M the embedding table holds 168M of 268M parameters, and because quantisation methods leave the embedding in 16-bit, it is also most of the file once the rest of the model is compressed. We ask what that table actually buys.\nL17: We replace it with one frozen table of 512-bit codes, 16 MB, with no trainable parameters, derived once from a pretrained embedding by principal-component projection and sign binarisation, and we train the same Gemma-shaped body from scratch on the same 2.05B tokens with and without it. The learned table buys 0.20 nats of validation loss for 2.6 times the trainable parameters, but on LAMBADA, HellaSwag and PIQA the frozen-table model is better or tied; only ARC-Easy favours the learned table.\nL18: With a ternary BitNet b1.58 body the whole model is a 46 MB file, still ties the float model on LAMBADA and PIQA, and on a laptop CPU generates 615 to 740 tokens per second in 62 MB of memory, five to six times the speed of the float model's 8-bit GGUF in cite8†this http URL†llama.cpp at a seventh of the memory.\nL19: The frozen table wins by 1.7 to 2.3 nats on rare words, accepts 10,000 new words after training with a 3% cost where the learned table collapses by 43%, and all of it is released with the weights, the data and every evaluation answer.\nL20: Comments:  | 9 pages, 2 figures, 6 tables. Code, data, checkpoints and per-question results: cite9†this https URL†huggingface.co . Preprint DOI: cite10†https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23382634†doi.org L21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2610.22428 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2610.22428v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.22428†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Saikiran Bathula [cite14†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Thu, 15 Oct 2026 06:34:18 UTC (80 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Embeddings Are Overpriced? A Frozen 16 MB Vocabulary for Small and Ternary Language Models, by Sai Kiran Bathula\nL35: \nL36:   * cite6†View PDF L37:   * cite7†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite15†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * cite17†Audio Summary L45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.CL\nL48: \nL49: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L50: \nL51: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2026-10 L52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: cite23†cs L56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L60:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L61:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L62: \nL63: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL64: \nL65